<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-chain-conditions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Chain conditions</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We introduce in this section important classes of modules, viz., Artinian and Noetherian modules. In this section we assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity and <m>A</m> is a right <m>R</m>-algebra. For an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> we denote by <m>S(M)</m> the collection of all <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <definition xml:id="def-artinian-module">
        <title>Artinian Module</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is said to be <term>Artinian</term> if there are no infinite, strictly decreasing sequences of submodules of <m>M</m>, i.e., if <me>
                    N_1\supsetneq N_2\supsetneq N_3\supsetneq\cdots
                </me>
                is a decreasing chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> then, there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>N_i=N_j</m> for all <m>i,j\geq\ell</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition xml:id="def-noetherian-module">
        <title>Noetherian Module</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is said to be <term>Noetherian</term> if there are no infinite, strictly increasing sequences of submodules of <m>M</m>, i.e., if <me>
                    N_1\subsetneq N_2\subsetneq N_3\subsetneq\cdots
                </me>
                is a increasing chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> then, there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>N_i=N_j</m> for all <m>i,j\geq\ell</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        We often use the following equivalent conditions for Artinian/Noetherian modules.
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Following are equivalent for an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m>.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The module <m>M</m> is Noetherian (resp. Artinian);
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Every nonempty subset of <m>S(M)</m> has a maximal (resp., minimal) element.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
                Furthermore, <m>M</m> is Noetherian if and only if every <m>A</m>-submodule of <m>M</m> is finitely generated.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <example xml:id="ex-noetherian-but-not-artinian">
        <title>Noetherian but not Artinian</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we consider <m>\Z</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module then, <m>\Z</m> is Noetherian but not Artinian.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-artinian-but-not-noetherian">
        <title>Artinian but not Noetherian</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we consider <m>\Z(p^\infty)=\{a/p^n:a\in\Z,n\in\N\}</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module then, <m>\Z</m> is Artinian but not Noetherian.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-artinian-noetherian-module">
        <title>Module which is both Artinian and Noetherian</title>
        
       <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>V</m> be a finite-dimensional vector space over a field <m>F</m>. Due to dimension reasons, <m>V</m> is both Artinian and Noetherian <m>F</m>-module.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every finite abelian group as a <m>\Z</m>-module is both Artinian and Noetherian.
        </p>
       </statement> 
    </example>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-technical-condition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>P,Q,R</m> be <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> with <m>P\subseteq Q</m>. Then there exists the following exact sequence of <m>A</m>-modules.
                <me>
                    0\to Q\cap R\big/P\cap R\to Q/P\to Q+R\big/P+R\to 0
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-artinian-noetherian-short-exact-sequence">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>0\to N\to M\to P</m> be a short exact sequence of <m>A</m>-modules. The module <m>M</m> is Noetherian (resp. Artinian) if and only if <m>N</m> and <m>P</m> are Noetherian (resp. Artinian).
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-artinian-noetherian-implies-composition-series">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is both Artinian and Noetherian and that <m>M\neq 0</m>. Then, there exists a sequence of submodules of <m>A</m>
                <me>
                    0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_{n-1}\subsetneq M_n=M
                </me>
                such that <m>M_{i+1}/M_{i}</m> are simple <m>A</m>-modules for <m>i&lt;n</m>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            By the assumption we have <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M</m>. Consider the set <m>S_1</m> of all the nonzero submodules of <m>M</m>. As <m>M\in S_1</m>, the set <m>S_1\neq\emptyset</m>. As <m>M</m> is Artinian, using <xref ref="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element"/>, there exists a minimal element, say <m>M_1\in S_1</m>. Note that by the minimality <m>M_1/M_0\simeq M_1</m> is simple. We continue as before by considering the set of all submodules of <m>M</m> containing <m>M_1</m>, so as to obtain a submodule <m>M_2</m> with <m>M_2/M_1</m> simple. Continuing in this way one obtains a increasing chain of submodules
            <me>
                0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq M_2\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq 
            </me>
            such that <m>M_{i+1}/M_i</m> is simple. Now Noetherian hypothesis implies that there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>M_\ell=M_i</m> for <m>i\geq\ell</m>. It follows that <m>M_\ell=M</m>.
            
        </p>
    </proof>
    <definition xml:id="def-composition-series">
        <title>Composition series and composition factors</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                A chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>, <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_n=M</m>  is called a <term>composition series of <m>M</m></term> if <m>M_{i+1}/M_i</m> is simple for <m>i&lt;n</m>. The factor modules <m>M_{i+1}/M_i</m> are called <term>composition factors</term> of the series.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-composition-series-iff-artinian-noetherian">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A nonzero <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is both Artinian and Noetherian if and only if <m>M</m> has a composition series.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
        <p>
            We have proved in <xref ref="prop-artinian-noetherian-implies-composition-series"/> that if <m>M</m> is both Artinian and Noetherian then, <m>M</m> has a composition series.
        </p>
    </proof>
    
    <theorem xml:id="thm-Jordan-Holder-for-modules">
        <title>Jordan-Hölder Theorem</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Assume that <m>M</m> is an <m>A</m>-module. If <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq M_2\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_n=M</m> and <m>0=M'_0\subsetneq M'_1\subsetneq M'_2\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M'_k=M</m> are composition series of <m>M</m> then, <m>n=k</m> and there is a permutation <m>\sigma\in S_n</m> such that <m>M'_{j+1}/M'_j\simeq M_{\sigma(j+1)}/M_{\sigma(j)}</m> for all <m>j&lt;n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>n=1</m>, i.e., we have <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M_1=M</m> and that <m>M_1/M_0\simeq M_1</m> is simple. This shows that <m>M</m> can not have a nonzero proper submodule. Hence, <m>k=1</m>. Now we assume that <m>n\geq 2</m>. Consider the following chain of submodules of <m>M</m>.
            <md>
                <mrow> 0=M_0'\cap M_{n-1}\subseteq M_1'\cap M_{n-1}\subseteq\amp\cdots\subseteq M_k'\cap M_{n-1}=M_{n-1}</mrow>
                <mrow> \subseteq M_{n-1}=M'_0+M_{n-1}\subseteq M_1'+M_{n-1}\amp\subseteq\cdots\subseteq M_k'+M_{n-1}=M_n=M</mrow>
            </md>
            For <m>j&lt;k</m> we have the following exact sequence of <m>A</m>-modules (see <xref ref="lem-technical-condition"/>).
            <me>
                0\to \frac{M_{j+1}'\cap M_{n-1}}{M_j'\cap M_{n-1}}\to \frac{M_{j+1}'}{M_j'}\to \frac{M_{j+1}'+M_{n-1}}{M_j'+M_{n-1}}\to 0
            </me>
            As <m>\tfrac{M_{j+1}'}{M_j'}</m> is simple either
            <me>
                0=\frac{M_{j+1}'\cap M_{n-1}}{M_j'\cap M_{n-1}}\quad\text{and}\quad\frac{M_{j+1}'}{{M_j'}}\simeq \frac{M_{j+1}'+M_{n-1}}{M_j'+M_{n-1}}
            </me>
            or
            <me>
                0=\frac{M_{j+1}'+M_{n-1}}{M_j'+M_{n-1}}\quad\text{and}\quad\frac{M_{j+1}'}{M_j'}\simeq \frac{M_{j+1}'\cap M_{n-1}}{M_j'\cap M_{n-1}}.
            </me>
            Furthermore, <m>M_n/M_{n-1}</m> is simple implies that there exists exactly one <m>i&lt;k</m> such that <m>M_{n-1}=M_i'+M_{n-1}</m> and <m>M_n=M_{i+1}'+M_{n-1}</m>. Therefore, <m>M_n/M_{n-1}\simeq M_{i+1}'/M_i'</m> and 
            <me>
                0=M_0'\cap M_{n-1}\subsetneq M_1'\cap M_{n-1}\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_{i-1}'\cap M_{n-1}\subsetneq M_i'\cap M_{n-1}{\color{blue}=}M_{i+1}'\cap M_{n-1}'\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_k'\cap M_{n-1}
            </me>
            is a composition series for <m>M_{n-1}</m>. By the induction hypothesis, <m>k-1=n-1</m> and there is a permutation <m>\sigma'\in S_{n-1}</m> such that <m>M_{j+1}'/M_j'\simeq M_{\sigma'(j+1)}/M_{\sigma'(j)}</m> for all <m>j\neq i</m>. We define <m>\sigma\in S_n</m> as <m>\sigma(j)=\sigma'(j)</m> for <m>j\neq i</m> and <m>\sigma(i)=n-1</m>. This gives a required result.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <definition xml:id="def-length-of-composition-series">
        <statement>
            <p>
                We keep notations of <xref ref="def-composition-series"/>. By <xref ref="thm-Jordan-Holder-for-modules"/>, the number <m>n</m> is unique for any composition series of <m>M</m>. This number is called the <term>composition length of <m>M</m></term> and it is denoted by <m>\ell(M)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-additivity-of-length">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>M, N</m> and <m>P</m> are both Artinian and Noetherian <m>A</m>-modules. If <m>0\to N\to M\to P\to 0</m> is a short exact sequence of modules then, 
                <me>
                    \ell(N)+\ell(P)=\ell(M).
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>
    <p>
        We denote by <m>\omega</m> the first infinite ordinal number.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Following conditions are equivalent for a nonzero <em>semisimple</em> <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m>.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The module <m>M</m> is finitely generated as an <m>A</m>-module
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The module <m>M</m> is direct sum of finitely many simple modules.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The module <m>M</m> is Artinian.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The module <m>M</m> is Noetherian.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            There exists <m>m&lt;\omega</m> such that if <m>M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_k</m> is finite then, <m>k\leq m</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
        <p>
            We first show that (1) and (2) are equivalent. Suppose that <m>M</m> is a finitely generated semisimpe module. Let <m>M=\oplus_{i\in I}N_i</m> with <m>I</m> infinite. If <m>\{u_1,\ldots,u_r\}</m> are finite generators of <m>M</m> then, there exists <m>i_j</m> such that <m>u_j\in N_{i_j}</m>. For any <m>k&gt;\max\{i_1,\ldots,i_r\}</m>, an element <m>u_k\in N_k</m> is not <m>A</m>-linear combination of <m>\{u_{i_j}\}</m> because <m>M</m> is a direct sum of <m>N_i</m>'s. Therefore, <m>I</m> can not be infinite. Conversely, assume that <m>M</m> is a direct sum of finitely many simple <m>A</m>-modules, say <m>M=\oplus_{i=1}^mN_i</m>. By <xref ref="prop-equivalent-criterion-for-simple-modules"/>, there exists <m>u_i\in N_i</m> such that <m>u_iA=N_i</m> for each <m>i\in\{1,2,\ldots,m\}</m>. As <m>M=\oplus_{i=1}^mN_i=\oplus_{i=1}^mu_iA</m>, elements <m>u_1,u_2,\ldots,u_m</m> are finite number of generators of <m>M</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Now we show that (2) and (3) (resp., (2) and (4)) are equivalent. Assume that <m>M</m> is a direct sum of finitely many simple modules. Every simple module is both Artinian and Noetherian. Therefore, by applying <xref ref="lem-artinian-noetherian-short-exact-sequence"/> finitely many times, we get that <m>M</m> is Artinian and Noetherian. Conversely, suppose that <m>u_i</m> (<m>i\in I</m>) are infinite generators of <m>M</m>. We choose countable generators <m>u_{i_1},u_{i_2},\ldots</m> among <m>\{u_i\}</m>. Consider submodules <m>M_k=\langle u_{i_{k+1},u_{k+2},\ldots}\rangle</m> for <m>k\geq 0</m> (resp., <m>N_k=\langle u_{i_1},\ldots,u_{i_k}\rangle</m> for <m>k\geq 1</m>). Then, <m>M_0\supsetneq M_1\supsetneq M_2\supsetneq\cdots</m> (resp., <m>N_1\subsetneq N_2\subsetneq\cdots</m>) is an infinite decreasing (resp., increasing) chain of submodules of <m>M</m>. Therefore, <m>M</m> can not be Artinian (resp., Noetherian).
        </p>
    </proof>
    
</section>